Fatigue is one of the greatest challenges to maintaining an efficient account.

Avoiding Fatigue
- Active testing. 20-40 new videos tested each month. Launching sub 15 videos should not be a sustained trend. ensure you're testing based on what you've learned works. launching new vidos in the wrong style will cause your account to slide.
- Iterate based on wins, don't settle once wins are unlocked. When creative refreshes stall, there is more room for assets to grow stagnant. A proven video tends to hold for 1-2 months at scale, then it begins to get more expensive with fatigue (1.5-2x higher CPAs)
- Keep an eye on volume as a ratio of spend. Ad ratios are meaningless if you're producing the wrong formats. On average, run 9-13 ads per $100k but the number of winners per $100k makes or breaks performance. Healthy performance typically requires 10+ winners per $100k, taking up 90% of spend. Less than 2 winners per $100K can drive CPAs up 40-60%. Recovery can start with 4-5 winners per $100k but you should aim for the gold standard if you want to maintain efficiency. 
- Manage the spend spread across active videos. Keep 3-4 low scale winners in the mix to help with spend distribution, and try to unlock new wins to avoid crowding one low CPA video into inefficiency. Avoid having any video spend 25%+ of budgeet. The winner ratio also matters here, because fewer winners causes spend to pile into one ad. 
- move videos with early signs of fatigue into a new ad group and pause them in the old ad group. set up a way to monitor for these flags

Spotting Fatigue 
Referncing weekly ad data, certain metric trends can highlight fatiguing creatives--before CPAs rise.
- Spend begins to weight more heavily into a couple/handful of videos. As a video gets a higher % of spend, the chance of fatiguing increases exponentially. In the healthiest months, the top video might have 12-25% of total spend max; when it creeps to 35-45%, it's a sign of the times. The exception being a new video taking 30% of spend at a strong CPA from the start. Still, you know that won't hold.  
- CTR falls from its peak while cost per 1,000 impressions holds or rises. The same audience is seeing the ad again. You'll have as many as three consectuvei months of falling CTRs to signal that increasing CPAs are coming.If you notice a +30% CTR decrease at flat spend, you have about a month until that video is ready to be retired in that campaign/ad set (if it is relaunche,d it must be afains a new audience)
- Frequency rises above about 3–4 per user per week in Google Ads.
- Cost per acquisition drifts up while the campaign’s other ads hold steady. This is the signal that matters; the first two only explain it.
- The hook-hold rate drops: the share of viewers still watching after 5 seconds, from YouTube’s audience-retention report.
Summary: Its 4-week rolling cost per acquisition is 20% or more above its own best 4-week stretch, the campaign as a whole hasn’t moved, and frequency is rising. 

Navigating Fatigue
- Title updates (test a winning title format) 
- Limit to placements where the video did not serve previously
= Keep the script, but swap the hook and b-roll
- Update thumbnail
- Pruning
- Aim for 1 in every 3/4 ads to be a winner. You'll likely see poor performance if that ratio is 1/7. 
- Launch different creators
- Update creative mix (partnership ads vs. ugc)
- Videos may fail in one placements, but perform better in another. Slower storytelling can perform better on TV where it lags on Mobile. Consider this.
- Launch new videos in proven styles. When fighting fatigue, this is not necessarily the time to try a majority of net new concepts. Look at your top videos, and change enough (creator, length, products featured) that its meaningfully different while leaning on the same exact concept (ugc, unboxing, same tone and pacing or seasonal product highlight).
- Retest a proven winner. These should be ~5-10% above your campaign average when they were live. Wait a quarter to reactiate. A weak ad style/format will not return os a winner, and the failed concept needs deep work or a pivot.

